<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Moon Represents My Heart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">sold to Li Ge Records in <a href="/years/1972">1972</a>; first recorded by Chen Fen-lan, May <a href="/years/1973">1973</a>; Teresa Teng's version 19 December <a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 月亮代表我的心, Yuèliang Dàibiǎo Wǒ de Xīn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The music of &quot;The Moon Represents My Heart&quot; is by the Taiwanese composer Weng Ching-hsi. While studying at the Berklee College of Music in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, he often found ideas for his music in the nearby parks and wrote them down. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> he showed one of his compositions to his friend the lyricist Sun Yi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Weng Ching-hsi thought little of the tune and considered throwing it away; the lyricist Sun Yi found it in a pile of works meant for disposal and judged it a good song. After Sun wrote the words, the two sold it to Li Ge Records in 1972. Chen Fen-lan sang it first, on her album Dreamland in May 1973, and Liu Guan-lin recorded it in November of the same year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-teng">Teresa Teng</a> recorded it for her Mandarin album Love Songs of the Island, Vol. 4: Love in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, released by Polydor Hong Kong on 19 December 1977, and her version made it known across the Chinese-speaking world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Teng's recording lasts three minutes and 27 seconds and has been described as a love song with a waltz-like lilt. The anthropologist Kevin Latham called it a relatively simple love ballad, like most gangtai songs of the time, the popular music of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. The linguist Adrian Tien, in The Semantics of Chinese Music, notes that the moon has long served in Chinese culture to express sorrow over lost love or hope of reunion, and cites the song as a modern example of that image.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Foreign music had been kept out of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for decades, and romantic songs were condemned there as bourgeois and decadent. From the late 1970s Teng's songs, soft and restrained against the officially sanctioned revolutionary songs, spread widely, and this one became among the first popular gangtai songs in the country while the leaders of the People's Republic tried to suppress her music. The film director Jia Zhangke recalled it as something completely new, a personal world after a time when everything had been collective.</p><p>The song has been covered by many singers, among them Nana Mouskouri, Faye Wong, Leslie Cheung, Andy Lau, Siti Nurhaliza and Kenny G, and in 2019 the Museum of Chinese in America in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> staged an exhibition inspired by it. In 2026 it was played at the state dinner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a>'s state visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Sun Yi sued Li Ge Records over the rights and lost, so the company holds the copyright.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Moon Represents My Heart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
